  "notes": "The Hitmen captain, and the research gives him a tension the other Bad Boy entries do not have: major-key 70s/80s soul and disco kept in the source key, but \"he personally preferred grittier minor material, so his cuts sit darker than the label average\". That is authored literally — the scale set is major and natural minor at equal weight, the key set carries both, and a minor family (i–VI–VII) sits third among otherwise-complete pop progressions. The kit is the other parameter: \"big compressed kit\" with \"arena reverb\" and live percussion overdubs gives the highest `layerClapOffsetMs` on this axis (4–10 ms is what makes a clap read as wide), raised velocity tiers and four perc lanes at 2–4 hits a bar. The kick is on 1 and **3**, not 3.5. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high: the Hitmen method is documented first- and second-hand, the drum numbers are not. The specific inferred parameter is `drums.snare.layerClapOffsetMs` — \"arena reverb\" is a mix property this model translates into clap spread, which is the only lever a MIDI style model has for width. ⚠ The i–iv family is INFERRED; the research names I–V–vi–IV and I–vi–IV–V explicitly and only characterises the minor side as \"grittier\". ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 48 to 47 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 48 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1.",
